# ==============================================================================
# 🚀 HOW TO START THE SERVERS & SERVICES (RUN COMMANDS)
# ==============================================================================
# 1. START DATABASE (PostgreSQL 16 with pgvector on port 5432):
#    $ docker compose up -d postgres
#
# 2. START BACKEND API SERVER (NestJS on http://localhost:3000):
#    $ npm install
#    $ npm run start:dev
#    (Auto-runs migrations & seeds demo users/patients if DB_MIGRATIONS_RUN=true)
#
# 3. START FRONTEND WEB APP (Next.js 16 on http://localhost:3001):
#    $ cd apps/web-app && npm install && npm run dev
#
# 4. START LOCAL LLM & EMBEDDINGS (Ollama for 100% private HIPAA on-premise AI):
#    $ ollama serve
#    $ ollama pull llama3.2
#    $ ollama pull nomic-embed-text
#
# 5. RUN TESTING & QUALITY SUITES:
#    $ npm test                # Run all 44 test suites (606 unit/integration tests)
#    $ npm run test:eval       # Run AI system evaluation assertions against DB
# ==============================================================================

PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=development
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=hospital_user
DB_PASSWORD=change-me
DB_NAME=hospital_db
# Apply pending migrations automatically at start (set false to run `npm run migration:run` yourself)
DB_MIGRATIONS_RUN=true
JWT_SECRET=generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32-must-be-32-chars-min
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=8h
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3001
SEED_USER_PASSWORD=change-me-too
SEED_DEMO_DATA=true
FHIR_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8080/fhir
ORTHANC_URL=http://localhost:8042
ORTHANC_USERNAME=orthanc
ORTHANC_PASSWORD=change-me
WAIT_ALERT_MINUTES=30
# LLM: openai | google | groq  (the key for the chosen provider is required for /ai/*)
LLM_PROVIDER=openai
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# Embeddings: auto = OpenAI when OPENAI_API_KEY is set, otherwise local lexical hashing
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=ollama   # openai | ollama | auto

# --- Chat model: cli_proxy (default if unset) | anthropic | ollama | google | groq | openai ---
# Anthropic-compatible proxy:
CLI_PROXY_API_URL=
CLI_PROXY_API_TOKEN=
CLI_PROXY_MODEL=auto
# Local models (private, free; CPU is slow for answers that the model writes):
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2-hospital
OLLAMA_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=
GROQ_API_KEY=

# --- AI tuning (defaults shown) ---
AI_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
# Set false to bypass the L1/L2 rule router and send all questions directly to the Mastra AI Agent
ENABLE_ROUTER=false
AI_MAX_RETRIES=2
AI_RETRY_BASE_MS=500
AI_TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
AI_BREAKER_THRESHOLD=5
AI_BREAKER_COOLDOWN_MS=30000
AI_CONTEXT_TOKENS=3000
AI_WARMUP=true

# ==============================================================================
# 🏥 HOSPITAL AI AGENT SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & CONCEPTS GUIDE
# ==============================================================================
# This guide details all 21 AI engineering and clinical safety concepts
# implemented across this codebase: WHAT IT IS, WHERE IT IS IMPLEMENTED,
# WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED, HOW IT WAS IMPLEMENTED, and WHAT IS ITS USE.
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. 🧠 LLM (Model, Prompting, Reasoning)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   The core Large Language Model engine providing natural language comprehension,
#   clinical reasoning, and context-aware response synthesis.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/llm/model.ts
#   src/ai/llm/llm-provider.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   A unified, provider-agnostic model resolver with deterministic sampling settings.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Abstracts local Ollama (llama3.2-hospital), OpenAI (GPT-4o), Google Gemini 1.5,
#   and Groq through the Vercel AI SDK; sets low temperature (0.1) for reproducible
#   medical decision-making, with automated CLI proxy support.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Analyzes clinical data, interprets complex medical symptoms, drafts clinical
#   notes (SBAR, prior-auth), and drives conversational interactions.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. 📝 Structured Output (JSON / Schema-Based Responses)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Guarantees that model responses strictly follow typed JSON contracts rather than
#   unpredictable free-form prose.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/query/l2-planner.service.ts
#   src/ai/routing/vitals-parser.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Schema-enforced JSON generation with automated healing and deterministic parsing.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Uses Zod schemas (`planSchema`) passed to AI SDK `generateObject({ schema })`,
#   with regex fallback sanitizers and default object repair pipelines.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Ensures intent classification, entity extraction, and vital signs parsing can be
#   safely consumed by downstream code without formatting errors or runtime crashes.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. 🔧 Tools (Functions / APIs the Agent Can Use)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Executable TypeScript functions that give the AI agent read and write access
#   to hospital databases, medical records, and scheduling systems.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/tools/clinical-tools.factory.ts
#   src/ai/tools/tool-registry.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   A modular tool library exposing database operations, clinical workflows, and FHIR queries.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Built using the Mastra/AI SDK `tool()` interface with Zod parameter validation;
#   filtered at runtime against the authenticated user's role via `TOOL_ROLES`.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Enables the AI to fetch patient summaries, check specialist availability,
#   query vital trends, create lab orders, and book follow-up visits safely.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. 🔄 Tool Calling (Agent Decides Which Tool to Call)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   The autonomous decision loop where the LLM selects and invokes tools
#   dynamically based on the clinician's natural language request.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/agent.ts
#   src/ai/services/ai-answer.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Autonomous multi-step tool execution loop with recursive result evaluation.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Configured with `maxSteps: 5`; the model analyzes user intent, calls appropriate
#   tools with structured arguments, receives the execution output, and reasons over
#   the data before presenting a final answer.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Resolves complex multi-step queries like "Check if patient P1001's potassium is high,
#   and if so, check Dr. Chen's availability" in a single natural conversation turn.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. 🧠 Memory (Short / Long-Term Conversation Memory)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Multi-turn dialogue persistence that remembers previous statements, entities,
#   and context within a conversation thread.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/memory/conversation-memory.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Thread-isolated conversation storage in PostgreSQL with token-budget sliding windows.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Stores user and assistant turns in `AgentMessageEntity`; provides recent messages
#   (`ctx.recent`), generates automatic thread titles, and trims old history to fit
#   the model's context window.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Allows clinicians to ask natural follow-up questions ("what about her medications?",
#   "who is the first doctor you mentioned?") without having to retype context.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. 📦 Context (Manage What Information Goes to LLM)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   The pipeline that compiles, filters, and formats prompt context before invoking the model.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/context/agent-context.builder.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   A dynamic context aggregator with role-based filtering and SHA-256 state tracking.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Builds role-scoped system instructions, active patient vitals, emergency room alerts,
#   and current date/time; hashes the context (`hash`) to support audit logging and caching.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Ensures the LLM receives only necessary and authorized medical context, preventing
#   confidential patient data from leaking across clinical departments.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. 🔎 RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Grounds the AI's responses in verified medical guidelines, triage SOPs, and clinical
#   protocols by retrieving relevant passages from a vector database.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/rag/vector-rag.service.ts
#   src/ai/rag/pgvector-rag.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Automated knowledge ingestion, vector embedding, and semantic similarity search.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Indexes `/knowledge/*.md` files, hashes chunks (SHA-256) to skip re-indexing on restarts,
#   embeds text using Ollama (`nomic-embed-text`), stores vectors in PostgreSQL with `pgvector`,
#   and retrieves relevant chunks using cosine similarity (`<=>`).
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Guarantees that hospital policies (Code Blue, infection control, stroke triage) are
#   accurately cited, eliminating dangerous medical hallucinations.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. 🔗 MCP / External Connectors (Standard Way to Connect External Tools & Data)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Standardized connectors allowing the AI agent to interface with electronic health
#   records (EHR), picture archiving systems (PACS), and external healthcare services.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/fhir/fhir-hapi.client.ts
#   src/ai/dicom/orthanc.client.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   HL7 FHIR R4 standard REST client and DICOM Orthanc PACS imaging client.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Implements FHIR endpoints (`Patient`, `Observation`, `ImagingStudy`) on HAPI server
#   with circuit breaker protection, alongside DICOM REST connectors for radiological scans.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Ensures healthcare interoperability, allowing the hospital AI to integrate with
#   Epic, Cerner, and hospital imaging infrastructure.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. 🔀 Workflows (Control Multi-Step Execution & Human-in-the-Loop)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Deterministic orchestration of multi-step hospital actions with human approvals,
#   role authorizations, and read-back database verifications.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/actions/action-executor.service.ts
#   src/ai/actions/action-policy.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   A Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) proposal, approval, execution, and verification engine.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Agent outputs proposals marked as `AUTO` or `ASK_HUMAN` -> stored in PostgreSQL as `PENDING`
#   with expiration times -> licensed clinician approves -> status flips atomically to `EXECUTING`
#   -> executes action -> performs database read-back verification -> status flips to `DONE`.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Prevents autonomous AI from directly altering patient medical records or prescribing
#   medications without explicit clinician review and approval.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. 👥 Multi-Agent (Specialized Agents When Needed)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Architecture deploying dedicated, specialized agents tailored to specific hospital roles.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/agent.ts
#   src/ai/context/tool-policy.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Role-segregated agent factory generating 8 specialized hospital personas.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Instantiates agents for Doctor, Nurse, Receptionist, Radiologist, Pharmacist, Care Coordinator,
#   Specialist, and Admin; each agent receives a tailored system prompt and restricted tool access.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Enforces medical separation of duty: receptionists cannot view clinical diagnosis details,
#   and nurses cannot sign off on physician surgical referrals.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. 🛡️ Guardrails (Prevent Unsafe / Invalid Behavior)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Safety barriers that inspect LLM output to detect hallucinations, verify citations,
#   and intercept false or unauthorized claims before they reach clinicians.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/guardrails.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Pre-presentation checks for hallucinated IDs, false completion claims, and missing items.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   - `checkGuardrails`: Flags patient IDs not found in retrieved data or prompt.
#   - `reconcileActionClaims`: Intercepts claims like "I have booked" when no action was approved.
#   - `appendClosestPassage`: Recovers policy passages when LLM mistakenly claims "not found".
#   - `appendOmittedPatients`: Injects patient rows dropped by small context windows.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Eliminates clinical risks by ensuring clinicians are never misled by phantom medical IDs
#   or false confirmation of unperformed medical procedures.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. 🔐 Security (Permissions, Secrets, Prompt Injection, PHI)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Multi-layer healthcare security providing HIPAA-compliant PHI privacy, prompt
#   sanitization, and cryptographic API access control.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/security/phi-session.ts
#   src/security/jwt-auth.guard.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Bidirectional PHI de-identification and global Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   - `PhiSession`: Replaces patient names, phones, SSNs, and emails with stable tokens
#     (`[PATIENT_1]`) before sending to cloud LLMs, restoring them locally in the response.
#   - `JwtAuthGuard`: Validates cryptographically signed JWTs and enforces `@Roles(...)` metadata.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Protects patient privacy under HIPAA and GDPR, ensuring sensitive health data never
#   egresses to external cloud model providers or unauthorized staff.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 13. 🔁 Reliability (Retry, Timeout, Fallback, Recovery)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Resilience infrastructure protecting the application against network latency,
#   transient cloud errors, and external LLM outages.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/reliability/resilience.ts
#   src/ai/reliability/llm-runner.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Request timeouts, exponential backoff retries, and stateful circuit breakers.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   - `withTimeout`: Enforces strict execution limits (e.g., 45s for LLM, 8s for FHIR).
#   - `withRetry`: Retries transient 408/429/500+ errors with exponential backoff and jitter.
#   - `CircuitBreaker`: Trips open after repeated failures, failing fast to protect providers.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Ensures mission-critical reliability in emergency hospital departments, preventing hung
#   screens and providing graceful fallbacks during network disruptions.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 14. 🧪 Evals (Measure Quality)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Comprehensive automated testing framework that evaluates agent accuracy, factual
#   adherence, and role compliance across clinical scenarios.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   tests/evaluation/system/evaluator.ts
#   tests/evaluation/agent/harness.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   System-level assertions (149 checks) and role-based conversational test harnesses.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Tests the real dependency graph (Postgres, pgvector, PHI shield, memory, tools);
#   evaluates tool selection accuracy, proposal generation, and critical failure gates.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Quantifies agent clinical accuracy and ensures that new feature additions meet
#   strict hospital safety and diagnostic standards.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 15. 📈 Regression (Detect Quality Degradation)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Automated baseline comparison that detects any reduction in quality, reasoning,
#   or safety between software builds.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   tests/evaluation/system/evaluator.ts
#   tests/evaluation/baseline.json
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Continuous regression tracking comparing live test results against locked golden baselines.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Compares current test execution against `tests/evaluation/baseline.json`; exits with
#   a non-zero status code if any previously passing check fails, blocking the CI/CD pipeline.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Prevents model updates, prompt tuning, or database schema changes from silently breaking
#   existing clinical workflows or safety constraints.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 16. 🌊 Drift (Detect Behavior Changes)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Monitors semantic and statistical divergence in agent outputs or operational metrics over time.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   tests/evaluation/agent/semantic.ts
#   src/ai/observability/metrics.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Semantic cosine similarity testing and statistical anomaly detection in production.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   - `SemanticScorer`: Embeds model answers and clinical reference passages; flags a drift
#     warning if cosine similarity drops below 0.60.
#   - `detectDrift`: Compares production sliding windows against baselines for p95 latency spikes
#     (>= 2.5x), error rate increases (>= 10%), or tool call pattern shifts (>= 40%).
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Alerts clinical engineers when upstream LLM weight updates or prompt drifts cause the
#   agent to deviate from approved clinical consensus.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 17. 📝 Logging (Record Execution)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Auditing framework that records every agent interaction, step, decision, and system event.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/observability/run-recorder.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Step-level structured execution recording stored in PostgreSQL (`AgentRunEntity`).
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Captures user role, timestamp, model version, execution outcome, and step traces;
#   automatically strips and sanitizes PHI (`phi.maskDeep()`) before saving to disk.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Maintains an immutable, HIPAA-compliant audit trail for hospital compliance,
#   medical malpractice reviews, and system debugging.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 18. 👀 Observability (Monitor System Health)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Production monitoring telemetry tracking overall system health, error rates, and usage patterns.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/observability/run-recorder.ts
#   src/ai/observability/metrics.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Aggregated operational metrics calculation across hours and sliding windows.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Computes error rates (`errorRate`), guardrail warning frequencies (`guardrailWarnRate`),
#   tool failure percentages, and top utilized tools in real-time.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Provides hospital IT and DevOps administrators with real-time operational visibility
#   to ensure optimal uptime and prevent system bottlenecks.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 19. 🔍 Tracing (Follow Each Agent Execution)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Distributed span-level execution tracking showing the complete lifecycle of a user query.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/observability/run-recorder.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Hierarchical execution spans with start offsets and millisecond durations.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Assigns a unique `traceId` (UUID) per run; records nested execution spans
#   (`llm`, `memory`, `tool`) with exact execution start offsets (`startOffsetMs`) and durations.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Enables developers to inspect the exact execution waterfall and identify which tool,
#   database query, or LLM call caused latency or failure.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 20. 💰 Cost (Token / API Cost Monitoring)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Tracking and accounting of token volumes and API usage across model providers.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/observability/run-recorder.ts
#   src/ai/observability/metrics.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Per-request token recording and rolling average token consumption analytics.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Extracts prompt, completion, and total tokens from model responses (`usage`);
#   aggregates average tokens per run (`avgTokens`) for reporting.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Prevents unexpected cloud billing surprises, allows budget forecasting, and enables
#   departmental token allocation across hospital teams.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 21. ⚡ Performance (Latency and Throughput)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Instrumentation measuring system responsiveness, execution latency, and query throughput.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/observability/metrics.ts
#   src/ai/observability/run-recorder.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Statistical quantile analysis (p50 and p95 latency) and timing instrumentation.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Measures total execution duration (`durationMs`) per run; calculates p50 and p95 latency
#   percentiles across rolling windows to monitor real-world user response times.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Enforces strict healthcare Service Level Agreements (SLAs), guaranteeing rapid response
#   times critical for high-urgency clinical environments like emergency triage.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 22. 🚦 L1/L2 Query Router (Hybrid Fast Pattern & Semantic LLM Router)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Multi-tier query routing engine that inspects inbound questions to route them
#   either to sub-10ms deterministic handlers (L1), semantic LLM planners (L2),
#   or directly to the multi-turn conversational agent.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/query/query-router.service.ts
#   src/ai/query/l1-parser.ts
#   src/ai/query/l2-planner.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Hybrid two-tier router with confidence thresholding and bypass toggle (`ENABLE_ROUTER`).
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Uses regex and clinical lexicon tokenizers (<10ms). If confidence < 0.70, queries
#   the L2 model planner (`l2-planner.service.ts`). When `ENABLE_ROUTER=false` is set in .env,
#   the entire router is bypassed so all turns flow directly to the Mastra AI Agent.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Maximizes speed for high-frequency administrative lookups while providing full
#   conversational intelligence and follow-up memory for open-ended clinical questions.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 23. ⚡ Real-Time WebSocket Events (Live Hospital Event Streaming)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Full-duplex WebSocket event gateway that streams live hospital telemetry,
#   emergency alerts, and AI action lifecycle transitions to clinician dashboards without polling.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/events/hospital-events.gateway.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Role-authenticated Socket.IO gateway with selective room delivery.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Validates JWT bearer tokens on handshake; binds connections to role rooms (`role:${role}`)
#   and user rooms (`user:${userId}`); emits `agent.action.proposed`, `alert.news2_deterioration`,
#   `alert.critical_lab`, and `referral.created`.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Immediately notifies doctors and nurses of acute patient deterioration and updates
#   action approval cards in real time without requiring browser refreshes.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 24. 🏥 Clinical Decision Support (NEWS2 Score & Risk Triage)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Physiological scoring algorithm implementing the UK Royal College of Physicians
#   National Early Warning Score 2 (NEWS2), SIRS screen, and Emergency Severity Index (ESI).
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/news2.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Multi-parameter vital signs evaluation and acute risk stratification engine.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Evaluates respiration rate, SpO2, supplemental oxygen, systolic BP, pulse, consciousness
#   (AVPU), and temperature; scores from 0 to 20 and flags clinical escalation if score >= 5
#   or any single parameter scores 3 (red trigger).
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Early detection of sepsis and acute clinical deterioration; triggers automated
#   escalation alerts on hospital tracking boards to save lives.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 25. 💊 Pharmacological Decision Support & Medication Safety Gate
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Automated pharmacological safety screening system auditing proposed prescriptions
#   and consultation plans before physician signing.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/medication-safety.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Allergy class verification, drug-drug interaction detection, and renal eGFR contraindication checks.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Normalizes drug names; checks allergy cross-reactivity (penicillins, cephalosporins, NSAIDs);
#   detects dangerous combinations (Warfarin + NSAID, PDE5 + Nitrates); calculates CKD-EPI 2021
#   eGFR to contraindicate renally cleared drugs (e.g. Metformin if eGFR < 30).
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Prevents adverse drug events (ADEs), fatal drug-drug interactions, and contraindicated
#   prescriptions across inpatient and outpatient care.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 26. 🔄 Cross-Departmental Clinical Referrals Workflow
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Formal medical consultation management system that coordinates clinical handoffs
#   between attending physicians and hospital specialists.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/referrals/referral.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Referral lifecycle state machine (`REQUESTED` -> `ACCEPTED` -> `IN_PROGRESS` -> `COMPLETED`).
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Tracks urgency levels (`EMERGENCY`, `URGENT`, `ROUTINE`), AI evidence, and doctor notes;
#   notifies assigned specialists via WebSockets; upon completion, automatically advances
#   the patient's visit to `FOLLOW_UP`.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Eliminates lost referrals, prevents communication breakdowns between departments,
#   and maintains complete clinical accountability.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 27. 📅 Specialist Availability & Appointment Scheduling Engine
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Real-time doctor calendar and appointment booking engine that calculates available slots
#   and resolves scheduling conflicts.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/specialists/specialist.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Shift window evaluation, automated slot generation, and double-booking guards.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Models weekly shift windows (`DoctorAvailabilityEntity`); dynamically computes open slots;
#   asserts bookability (`assertBookable`) and prevents double-booking via 409 Conflict guards.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Enables immediate appointment scheduling during patient triage, consultations,
#   and follow-up care planning without scheduling collisions.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 28. 🧬 Patient Identity Resolution & De-duplication
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Deterministic patient matching engine that links records and catches duplicate
#   registrations during intake.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/patient-matching.ts
#   src/hospital/person-resolution.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Multi-factor scoring algorithm evaluating normalized names, phone numbers, and dates of birth.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Uses Unicode normalization (`NFKD`), phone suffix matching (>= 7 digits), and DOB agreement;
#   ranks match strength (`HIGH` vs `MEDIUM`) and provides non-blocking advice to front-desk staff.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Prevents fragmented medical records, ensuring patient medical histories, lab results,
#   and allergies are unified under a single clinical identity.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 29. 🛡️ Indirect Prompt Injection Defense & Data Fencing
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 29. 🛡️ Indirect Prompt Injection Defense & Data Fencing
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Security boundary preventing untrusted clinical text from overriding model rules
#   or executing unauthorized actions.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/data-fence.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   XML data enclosure (`<DATA source="...">`) and active tokenizer marker sanitization.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Wraps all retrieved patient records and tool outputs in `<DATA>` tags; strips out
#   adversarial role markers (`system:`, `assistant:`) and special tokenizer tags (`<|...|>`).
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Guarantees that malicious instructions embedded within clinical charts or referral notes
#   cannot hijack the AI agent or exfiltrate private patient data.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 30. 🔒 Dynamic Context Fencing & Patient Bound Checks
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Runtime execution policy that prevents agents from operating on discharged/closed
#   visits or targeting patients outside the active conversation thread.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/context/tool-policy.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   State-based tool withholding (`withheldReason`) and patient-bound argument guards (`bindingViolation`).
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Withholds consultation/booking tools if a patient's visit status is not open; blocks any
#   tool call targeting a `patientId` not in `ctx.allowedPatientIds` with a clear refusal message.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Prevents catastrophic cross-patient chart contamination where the AI might accidentally
#   prescribe or book for Patient B while chatting about Patient A.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 31. 📄 Dynamic Knowledge Ingestion, Multi-Format PDF Parsing & Atomic Indexing
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Live document upload and vector indexing pipeline accepting PDFs, Markdown, and TXT files.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/rag/knowledge-documents.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Multi-format stream extraction, binary PDF parsing (`pdf-parse`), and atomic pgvector ingestion.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Parses binary PDF or markdown buffers; stores content in PostgreSQL (`KnowledgeDocumentEntity`);
#   immediately generates vector embeddings; rolls back both database entity and vector chunks
#   if indexing fails to avoid inconsistent states.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Allows hospital directors to upload new emergency triage guidelines or hospital policies
#   and make them instantly searchable by the AI agent on the very next query.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 32. ⏱️ Background Queue Monitor & Emergency SLA Escalation Daemon
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Automated background daemon monitoring waiting room queues and enforcing emergency triage SLAs.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/queue-monitor.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Periodic SLA breach scanner with deduplicated real-time WebSocket alerts.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Runs recurring sweeps every 60s; detects visits exceeding wait limits (`WAIT_ALERT_MINUTES=30`);
#   emits `events.emitUnconsultedAlert` to emergency staff consoles; persists `waitAlertedAt`
#   to prevent repetitive alarms.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Eliminates waiting room clinical neglect, ensuring deteriorating patients left waiting
#   are immediately surfaced to triage nurses.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 33. 🔒 HIPAA Minimum Necessary Rule & Field-Level Access Matrix
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Granular field-level security enforcing the HIPAA 45 CFR § 164.502(b) Minimum Necessary standard.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/section-access.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Deterministic role-to-chart-section access matrix (`SECTION_ACCESS`).
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Restricts visibility of conditions, allergies, and medications based on role (e.g. pharmacists
#   see medications and allergies; receptionists see neither); applied identically across REST APIs
#   and AI prompt context builders.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Guarantees that non-clinical hospital staff never view confidential clinical diagnoses,
#   complying with medical privacy legislation.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 34. 📬 Decoupled Post-Commit Hooks & Resilience Pattern
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Distributed resilience pattern executing secondary side effects strictly after authoritative DB commits.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/post-commit.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Safe async executor containing downstream failures without rolling back primary clinical charts.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Wraps side-effect calls (external HAPI FHIR sync, search indexing); if downstream services fail,
#   logs errors and flags records (`fhirSynced: false`) while keeping local clinical records intact.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Ensures local electronic health records are never blocked, delayed, or rolled back due to
#   external EHR network hiccups or indexing delays.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 35. 📜 HIPAA Access Audit & Non-Repudiation Logging Interceptor
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Enterprise audit interceptor implementing the HIPAA §164.312(b) Audit Controls standard.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/security/audit.interceptor.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Automated request/response interceptor logging every access and mutation to protected health data.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Intercepts HTTP calls via NestJS `NestInterceptor` and RxJS `tap()`; records user ID, role,
#   HTTP verb, resource path, patient ID, and status code into `AuditLogEntity`.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Delivers non-repudiation forensic evidence required for regulatory HIPAA compliance,
#   internal security audits, and medical investigations.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 36. 📋 Comprehensive 4-Tier Follow-Up Care Bundles
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Multi-component post-consultation care package coordinating complex post-discharge plans.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/database/entities/follow-up-plan.entity.ts
#   src/hospital/hospital-lifecycle.service.ts
#   src/ai/actions/action-executor.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   4-component composite action executor handling appointments, lab orders, tasks, and patient notices.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   When approved, the executor books follow-up appointments, places repeat laboratory orders,
#   creates nursing check-in tasks, and sends patient care instructions in a single coordinated workflow.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Reduces 30-day hospital readmission rates by automating discharge planning and ensuring
#   continuity of clinical care.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 37. 🚨 Critical Panic Lab Value Surveillance & Auto-Task Creation
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   Automated diagnostic safety system screening incoming laboratory results for life-threatening panic values.
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/hospital/lab-rules.ts
#   src/hospital/labs/lab-orders.service.ts
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   Panic threshold rule evaluator with instant clinician escalation tasks.
# • HOW IMPLEMENTED:
#   Compares numerical lab results against critical boundaries (e.g., Potassium < 2.5 or > 6.0 mmol/L,
#   elevated troponin); automatically dispatches urgent priority tasks to attending physicians and emits WebSocket alerts.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Guarantees immediate medical intervention for critical acute emergencies (e.g. fatal arrhythmias,
#   myocardial infarctions) before diagnostic delays cause patient harm.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 38. 🗄️ Database Architecture: Unified PostgreSQL + pgvector vs. ChromaDB / Pinecone
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# • WHAT IT IS:
#   A unified enterprise data architecture where relational clinical charts,
#   audit records, and high-dimensional AI vector embeddings share the same
#   PostgreSQL 16 database engine, completely eliminating external vector stores (ChromaDB).
# • WHERE IMPLEMENTED:
#   src/ai/rag/pgvector-rag.service.ts
#   src/database/entities/
# • WHAT WAS IMPLEMENTED:
#   PostgreSQL `pgvector` extension with HNSW cosine indexes, native SQL vector distance
#   calculations (`<=>`), and Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) hybrid search.
# • WHY WE AVOID CHROMADB:
#   1. Zero Dual-Database Desynchronization: When patient data is modified or deleted,
#      an external ChromaDB requires two-phase commits that easily fall out of sync.
#   2. Single ACID Transactions: Cascading deletions (`DELETE FROM patients WHERE id = $1`)
#      instantly and atomically purge clinical vector embeddings in the exact same commit.
#   3. Native SQL Foreign-Key Joins & Metadata Filters: Fast relational queries joining
#      patient demographics with vector similarity without pulling IDs into Node.js memory.
#   4. Hybrid Search via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF): Combines PostgreSQL full-text search
#      (`to_tsvector`, `ts_rank_cd`) and dense vector similarity into a single SQL pass.
#   5. HIPAA & Operational Simplicity: Only one database service (PostgreSQL on port 5433)
#      to backup, encrypt, audit, and maintain—eliminating extra server costs and network latency.
# • WHAT IS THE USE:
#   Guarantees bulletproof clinical data integrity, zero desynchronization lag, and sub-millisecond
#   RAG knowledge retrieval for hospital AI agents.



# ==============================================================================
# 📐 SOLID DESIGN PRINCIPLES IMPLEMENTATION GUIDE
# ==============================================================================
# Demonstrates how the 5 foundational Object-Oriented design principles are
# strictly enforced across the hospital codebase:
#
# 1. 🔹 S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)
#    • Every service and module has one, and only one, reason to change:
#      - News2Service (`src/hospital/news2.service.ts`): Responsible solely for
#        physiological vital score mathematics (NEWS2, SIRS, ESI). Has zero DB
#        or network dependencies; pure domain logic.
#      - MedicationSafetyService (`src/hospital/medication-safety.service.ts`):
#        Dedicated purely to pharmacological allergy cross-matching, drug-drug
#        interactions, and renal eGFR formulas.
#      - PhiSession (`src/ai/security/phi-session.ts`): Solely responsible for
#        in-memory tokenization and de-identification of Protected Health Information.
#      - RunRecorder (`src/ai/observability/run-recorder.ts`): Only tracks execution
#        spans, latency timings, and token metrics for a single request lifecycle.
#
# 2. 🔹 O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
#    • Open for extension, closed for modification:
#      - EmbeddingProvider (`src/ai/rag/embedding.provider.ts`): New embedding
#        backends (e.g. Cohere, AWS Bedrock) can be added by extending the abstract
#        class without changing `VectorRagService` or vector retrieval logic.
#      - ClinicalToolsFactory (`src/ai/tools/clinical-tools.factory.ts`): New clinical
#        tools are added with independent Zod schemas and role policies without modifying
#        the agent execution harness.
#      - ActionPolicy (`src/ai/actions/action-policy.ts`): New clinical action types
#        are registered via policy maps without modifying existing execution branches.
#
# 3. 🔹 L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)
#    • Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without breaking the program:
#      - EmbeddingProvider Implementations (`src/ai/rag/embedding.provider.ts`):
#        `OpenAiEmbeddingProvider`, `OllamaEmbeddingProvider`, and `LocalHashEmbeddingProvider`
#        all conform to `embed(texts: string[]): Promise<number[][]>` and can be swapped
#        transparently with zero caller code changes.
#      - MockLanguageModelV3 in Unit Tests: Replaces live Ollama or OpenAI models
#        in `tests/evaluation/system/evaluator.ts` while adhering to the exact
#        Vercel AI SDK LanguageModel contract.
#
# 4. 🔹 I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
#    • Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use:
#      - SpecialistView (`src/hospital/specialists/specialist.service.ts`): Exposes
#        only public directory fields (`id`, `name`, `specialty`, `isAvailable`)
#        to clinical staff, hiding sensitive user properties like password hashes.
#      - SafetyPatient (`src/hospital/medication-safety.service.ts`): Requires only
#        age, gender, allergies, and current medications—decoupled from visit IDs
#        or billing entities.
#      - RunOutcome & RouteTrace: Segregated DTOs containing only the minimal telemetry
#        needed for tracing rather than exposing entire raw request objects.
#
# 5. 🔹 D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
#    • High-level modules depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations:
#      - Inversion of Control (IoC): NestJS constructor dependency injection across
#        all services (`VectorRagService`, `ActionExecutorService`, `AiService`).
#      - VectorRagService depends on abstract `EmbeddingProvider` and abstract
#        `VectorStore` interfaces rather than direct HTTP client calls.
#      - LlmRunner (`src/ai/reliability/llm-runner.ts`) wraps resilience around
#        generic async execution lambdas (`() => Promise<T>`), completely agnostic
#        of specific vendor SDKs.

# ==============================================================================
# 🏛️ SYSTEM DESIGN & ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE CONCEPTS GUIDE
# ==============================================================================
# Highlights the high-level distributed systems and architectural patterns
# implemented across this production-grade medical solution:
#
# 1. 🏗️ Clean / Layered Hexagonal Architecture:
#    • Presentation Layer: REST Controllers (`AiController`, `HospitalController`),
#      WebSockets (`HospitalEventsGateway`), React/Next.js UI.
#    • Application Layer: Orchestrators (`AiService`, `HospitalLifecycleService`,
#      `ActionExecutorService`, `ReferralService`).
#    • Domain Layer: Pure business logic (`news2.service.ts`, `medication-safety.service.ts`,
#      `availability.ts`, `data-fence.ts`).
#    • Infrastructure Layer: TypeORM entities, PostgreSQL `pgvector`, HAPI FHIR client,
#      Orthanc DICOM client.
#
# 2. ⚡ Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) & Reactive Streaming:
#    • Full-duplex Socket.IO gateway (`HospitalEventsGateway`) emits domain events
#      (`agent.action.proposed`, `alert.news2_deterioration`, `alert.critical_lab`,
#      `referral.created`) to role-authorized WebSocket rooms.
#    • Keeps clinician screens synchronized with zero polling latency.
#
# 3. 🛡️ Circuit Breaker Pattern (Fault Tolerance & Anti-Fragility):
#    • Stateful 3-phase circuit breaker (`CLOSED` -> `OPEN` -> `HALF_OPEN`) implemented
#      in `src/ai/reliability/resilience.ts`.
#    • Intercepts repeated external service timeouts (FHIR, DICOM, Cloud LLMs) and fails
#      fast after 5 errors, preventing thread starvation and cascading system crashes.
#
# 4. ⏳ Distributed Rate Limiting & Throttling:
#    • Configured via `@nestjs/throttler` in `AppModule` (120 req/min per IP/token).
#    • Protects healthcare infrastructure from denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks and
#      runaway cloud LLM token consumption.
#
# 5. 🔄 Idempotent State Machine & Atomic Claims:
#    • `ActionExecutorService` uses atomic conditional SQL updates:
#      `UPDATE agent_action SET status = 'EXECUTING' WHERE id = :id AND status = 'PENDING'`
#    • Eliminates race conditions and prevents double-execution when multiple clinicians
#      or dual socket/chat clicks attempt simultaneous approvals.
#
# 6. 🔍 Read-Back Verification Pattern:
#    • After executing database mutations (e.g. creating patient, booking appointment,
#      recording consultation), the executor re-queries the database independently to
#      verify the record truly exists before returning a success status.
#
# 7. 🗄️ CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation):
#    • Query Pipeline: High-speed, read-optimized paths (L1 pattern parser, vector RAG
#      cosine index, cached directory lookups) return sub-50ms responses.
#    • Command Pipeline: Formal Human-in-the-Loop proposal generation, strict RBAC
#      role verification, database transactions, and persistent audit logging.
#
# 8. 🔒 Defense-in-Depth & Zero Trust Security:
#    • Perimeter: Global `JwtAuthGuard` authenticating cryptographically signed tokens.
#    • Endpoint Level: `@Roles(...)` metadata verifying hospital clinical credentials.
#    • Tool Level: `TOOL_ROLES` access matrix restricting tool exposure per persona.
#    • Data Level: `data-fence.ts` XML isolation neutralizing indirect prompt injections.
#    • Egress Level: Bidirectional `PhiSession` de-identification shielding patient data.
#
# 9. 📊 Distributed Tracing & Span Telemetry:
#    • `RunRecorder` assigns a distributed UUID `traceId` per request, recording
#      hierarchical sub-millisecond execution spans (`llm`, `memory`, `tool`) with
#      start offsets, durations, token counts, and sanitized arguments.
#
# 10. 🧠 Two-Tier Caching & Deduplication:
#     • SHA-256 chunk hashing in `VectorRagService` skips unchanged Markdown documents
#       on startup, reducing cold-start indexing time from seconds to milliseconds.
#     • In-memory 30-second cache in `PhiAnonymizerService` avoids querying thousands
#       of patient names on every conversation turn.
#
# 11. 🤝 Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) Medical Safety Gate:
#     • High-risk actions (consultations, prescriptions, specialist referrals, lab orders)
#       are classified as `ASK_HUMAN`, generating pending proposal cards requiring explicit
#       physician review, sign-off, or clinical override notes before execution.
# ==============================================================================


